::JOUR_U04_UA_Q01::
Question ID: JOUR_U04_UA_Q01
[MLA.JOUR.MED.01] Source: Student Media Critique File. Topic: A school proposal to replace printed club flyers with a weekly digital activity board. News Brief: The activities office is testing a digital board that lists club meetings, tryout dates, volunteer events, and academic team deadlines. The board would appear on hallway screens and the school website every Monday. Support Argument: Supporters say the digital board would reduce paper waste, update quickly, and help students find information in one place. They point to last month, when three club flyers had outdated room numbers after schedule changes. Opposing Argument: Opponents say printed flyers are easier to notice during passing time and better for students who do not check the website. They argue that a digital board may be ignored if it contains too much information at once. Media Features and Language: The draft package uses the headline One Board, Every Opportunity, a sidebar titled What Changes Monday, a screenshot caption, and the phrase a clearer path through the noise of school announcements. Question 1: Which media feature best supports quick audience use? {
~A. The headline supports quick use because headlines are always complete directions.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
=B. The What Changes Monday sidebar supports quick audience use by separating practical updates.#Correct. Step 1: identify the media literacy skill in the standard. Step 2: use the embedded source to connect structure, central idea, purpose, claim, evidence, rhetoric, mood, or audience effect to the best answer. This answer is strongest because it explains the source rather than relying on personal preference.
~C. The screenshot caption cannot support readers because captions are decorative.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
~D. The sidebar is unrelated because sidebars are not media features.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
}

::JOUR_U04_UA_Q02::
Question ID: JOUR_U04_UA_Q02
[MLA.JOUR.MED.02] Source: Student Media Critique File. Topic: A school proposal to replace printed club flyers with a weekly digital activity board. News Brief: The activities office is testing a digital board that lists club meetings, tryout dates, volunteer events, and academic team deadlines. The board would appear on hallway screens and the school website every Monday. Support Argument: Supporters say the digital board would reduce paper waste, update quickly, and help students find information in one place. They point to last month, when three club flyers had outdated room numbers after schedule changes. Opposing Argument: Opponents say printed flyers are easier to notice during passing time and better for students who do not check the website. They argue that a digital board may be ignored if it contains too much information at once. Media Features and Language: The draft package uses the headline One Board, Every Opportunity, a sidebar titled What Changes Monday, a screenshot caption, and the phrase a clearer path through the noise of school announcements. Question 2: Which detail best supports the digital board argument? {
~A. The digital board argument is supported only because screens are modern.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
~B. The outdated room-number example proves printed flyers never work.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
~C. The digital board argument is unsupported because all updates are confusing.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
=D. The outdated room-number example supports the argument that a digital board can update information quickly.#Correct. Step 1: identify the media literacy skill in the standard. Step 2: use the embedded source to connect structure, central idea, purpose, claim, evidence, rhetoric, mood, or audience effect to the best answer. This answer is strongest because it explains the source rather than relying on personal preference.
}

::JOUR_U04_UA_Q03::
Question ID: JOUR_U04_UA_Q03
[MLA.JOUR.MED.03] Source: Student Media Critique File. Topic: A school proposal to replace printed club flyers with a weekly digital activity board. News Brief: The activities office is testing a digital board that lists club meetings, tryout dates, volunteer events, and academic team deadlines. The board would appear on hallway screens and the school website every Monday. Support Argument: Supporters say the digital board would reduce paper waste, update quickly, and help students find information in one place. They point to last month, when three club flyers had outdated room numbers after schedule changes. Opposing Argument: Opponents say printed flyers are easier to notice during passing time and better for students who do not check the website. They argue that a digital board may be ignored if it contains too much information at once. Media Features and Language: The draft package uses the headline One Board, Every Opportunity, a sidebar titled What Changes Monday, a screenshot caption, and the phrase a clearer path through the noise of school announcements. Question 3: Which opposing claim should the package address? {
=A. The package should address the concern that some students may ignore the website or miss printed hallway reminders.#Correct. Step 1: identify the media literacy skill in the standard. Step 2: use the embedded source to connect structure, central idea, purpose, claim, evidence, rhetoric, mood, or audience effect to the best answer. This answer is strongest because it explains the source rather than relying on personal preference.
~B. The package should ignore opponents because the support claim has a better headline.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
~C. The package should address lunch rules instead of the source issue.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
~D. The opposing claim proves the digital board must be rejected.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the relevant claim, feature, evidence, language, or appeal. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misstates the source, ignores evidence, confuses topic with central idea, overstates a claim, or adds unsupported information.
}

